Take care of yourself, okay?

36
00:02:07,080 --> 00:02:07,640
Thanks once again.

37
00:02:11,340 --> 00:02:12,940
Hey, where's my wallet?

38
00:02:16,380 --> 00:02:17,480
All right, we're back.

39
00:02:17,680 --> 00:02:25,020
So you probably heard a lot of technical words that are specifically related to the courtroom, to lawyers, and the laws.

40
00:02:25,560 --> 00:02:28,260
So why don't we take a look at some of those now in Language Takeaway.

41
00:02:30,210 --> 00:02:31,150
Language Takeaway.

42
00:02:32,330 --> 00:02:36,590
The first Language Takeaway we're looking at today is all about people.

43
00:02:36,950 --> 00:02:38,770
So people you will find in a courtroom.

44
00:02:39,290 --> 00:02:41,050
Who's the first person on our list, Marco?

45
00:02:41,250 --> 00:02:43,130
So the top person would be the judge.

46
00:02:44,710 --> 00:02:48,390
Now you didn't really see this word judge in the dialogue, right?

47
00:02:48,950 --> 00:02:51,250
You did hear though the phrase, Your Honor.

48
00:02:51,670 --> 00:02:52,090
Your Honor.

49
00:02:52,800 --> 00:02:53,760
So Your Honor.

50
00:02:54,360 --> 00:02:55,700
So that's how you talk to a judge.

51
00:02:56,600 --> 00:02:59,600
You always add Your Honor before it or something like that?

52
00:02:59,600 --> 00:03:00,600
That's right.

53
00:03:00,600 --> 00:03:04,840
So you know we say Mr. President when we're talking to Barack Obama.

54
00:03:04,840 --> 00:03:09,200
But if we're talking to a judge, he's a special person in American society.

55
00:03:09,200 --> 00:03:11,390
So we call him or her Your Honor.

56
00:03:11,760 --> 00:03:12,070
Your Honor.

57
00:03:12,760 --> 00:03:13,760
Okay.

58
00:03:13,760 --> 00:03:14,760
So we have the judge.

59
00:03:14,760 --> 00:03:15,760
That's our first person.

60
00:03:15,760 --> 00:03:16,190
Who else do we have?

61
00:03:16,880 --> 00:03:24,670
So we have the judge and now we have a group of 12 people who will ultimately decide if the person is guilty or not.

62
00:03:25,400 --> 00:03:26,400
That's right.

63
00:03:26,400 --> 00:03:27,400
So these people are normal.

64
00:03:27,400 --> 00:03:33,840
They come from all different parts of society and they will decide what to do with this person.

65
00:03:33,840 --> 00:03:34,660
Is he guilty or is she innocent?

66
00:03:35,560 --> 00:03:36,920
This group is called the jury.

67
00:03:38,160 --> 00:03:48,960
So the jury is a group of normal citizens who will listen to both sides of the story and will then decide if the person committed the crime or didn't commit the crime.

68
00:03:49,280 --> 00:03:49,420
That's right.

69
00:03:50,280 --> 00:03:51,280
A jury.

70
00:03:51,280 --> 00:03:53,220
And the big question is, who's fighting?

71
00:03:53,760 --> 00:03:54,460
We have two sides.

72
00:03:54,940 --> 00:03:58,400
Someone who is upset and someone who is defending themselves.

73
00:03:58,880 --> 00:04:00,980
The person who is upset, this is the prosecution.

74
00:04:01,580 --> 00:04:01,960
Okay.

75
00:04:02,300 --> 00:04:07,180
So the prosecution has a group of lawyers that are called the prosecutors, right?

76
00:04:07,360 --> 00:04:07,680
That's right.

77
00:04:07,940 --> 00:04:10,720
And they're trying to prove that that person committed the crime.

78
00:04:11,460 --> 00:04:15,280
For example, I say that Marco burned down my house.

79
00:04:16,120 --> 00:04:19,080
I go to court, I say I am the prosecution.

80
00:04:19,080 --> 00:04:21,000
I want him to pay me for my house.

81
00:04:21,420 --> 00:04:22,900
So I'm the prosecution.

82
00:04:23,300 --> 00:04:23,540
Okay.

83
00:04:23,600 --> 00:04:30,420
So you're trying to not only prove that I committed the crime, but also trying to get me to pay for the damage that I caused.

84
00:04:30,740 --> 00:04:31,120
Exactly.

85
00:04:31,480 --> 00:04:35,640
So in this case, the prosecution says, this man stole my wallet.

86
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:36,700
He's a pickpocket.

87
00:04:37,680 --> 00:04:39,000
And who's the pickpocket?

88
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:39,730
Or maybe the pickpocket.

89
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:41,480
Who's the other side of this?

90
00:04:41,480 --> 00:04:41,890
The defendant.

91
00:04:42,670 --> 00:04:43,530
Defend, defendant.

92
00:04:44,320 --> 00:04:45,800
Think about the word here.

93
00:04:45,800 --> 00:04:49,380
To defend is to stand up for yourself, to protect yourself.

94
00:04:49,380 --> 00:04:51,890
So the defendant says, listen, no, that's not true.

95
00:04:52,330 --> 00:04:53,310
I am not a pickpocket.

96
00:04:53,670 --> 00:04:54,530
I didn't do that.

97
00:04:54,810 --> 00:04:57,390
So we have the prosecution and the defense.

98
00:04:58,890 --> 00:05:04,610
And the person who might be guilty, the person who did the thing or didn't do the thing, is called the defendant.

99
00:05:05,720 --> 00:05:15,680
So in your example again about the crime that maybe I burnt your house down or I didn't, I would be the defendant and my lawyers would constitute my defense.

100
00:05:16,180 --> 00:05:17,300
Yeah, they are your defense.

101
00:05:17,840 --> 00:05:18,020
Okay.

102
00:05:18,840 --> 00:05:21,740
So very technical words, but the important thing to remember here is defendant.

103
00:05:22,240 --> 00:05:23,600
All right.

104
00:05:23,600 --> 00:05:23,850
Good.

105
00:05:24,600 --> 00:05:29,210
So now we have one key element that is also a person.

106
00:05:29,780 --> 00:05:31,270
We have the witness.

107
00:05:31,640 --> 00:05:35,960
All right, so the basic idea like you said is we have to tell a story.

108
00:05:35,960 --> 00:05:38,210
My story is that you burned down my house.

109
00:05:38,950 --> 00:05:39,830
And you say, no, I didn't.

110
00:05:40,190 --> 00:05:40,490
Prove it.

111
00:05:40,790 --> 00:05:41,770
I say, I can.

112
00:05:43,160 --> 00:05:44,790
Someone saw you with a lighter.

113
00:05:46,120 --> 00:05:47,250
So this person is my witness.

114
00:05:47,960 --> 00:05:50,850
That means that he or she saw what happened.

115
00:05:51,240 --> 00:05:54,310
So someone who sees a crime is a witness to that crime.

116
00:05:54,610 --> 00:05:55,810
Okay, very good.

117
00:05:56,610 --> 00:05:59,330
So a lot of people are in the courtroom.

118
00:05:59,690 --> 00:06:00,790
A lot of things are happening.

119
00:06:01,440 --> 00:06:13,840
So I think we should listen to the dialogue again and we'll be back to look at some more words on Language Takeaway Part 2.

120
00:06:13,840 --> 00:06:22,360
Your Honor, ladies and gentlemen of the jury, my client Robert Malone has been accused of a crime he did not commit.

121
00:06:22,360 --> 00:06:25,010
The prosecution has accused my client of being a pickpocket.

122
00:06:25,770 --> 00:06:29,010
I know we have heard the testimony of many people here today.

123
00:06:29,650 --> 00:06:32,850
People who claim the defendant, my client, stole their wallets.

124
00:06:33,810 --> 00:06:35,330
I feel sorry for these victims.

125
00:06:35,850 --> 00:06:36,590
I really do.

126
00:06:37,470 --> 00:06:38,970
But my client is innocent.

127
00:06:41,550 --> 00:06:42,610
Let's look at the facts.

128
00:06:43,680 --> 00:06:52,160
Number one, these so-called witnesses did not actually see the defendant steal anything.

129
00:06:52,160 --> 00:06:56,270
Number two, when the police stopped him, he did not have any of the stolen wallets.

130
00:06:57,920 --> 00:06:59,560
There is no evidence.

131
00:07:00,750 --> 00:07:05,830
Therefore, ladies and gentlemen of the jury, I ask you to think carefully before giving your verdict.

132
00:07:06,570 --> 00:07:07,250
Look at the facts.

133
00:07:08,210 --> 00:07:09,010
Look at the evidence.

134
00:07:10,330 --> 00:07:12,270
My client is innocent.

135
00:07:13,420 --> 00:07:16,620
Ladies and gentlemen of the jury, have you reached a verdict?

136
00:07:17,200 --> 00:07:18,180
Yes, Your Honor.

137
00:07:18,820 --> 00:07:20,460
Our verdict is not guilty.

138
00:07:21,160 --> 00:07:22,460
Thank you so much.

139
00:07:22,920 --> 00:07:23,480
You were great.

140
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:25,500
Thank you for all your hard work.

141
00:07:26,100 --> 00:07:27,120
You're welcome, Robert.

142
00:07:27,440 --> 00:07:30,320
I knew you were innocent, so my job was easy.

143
00:07:30,320 --> 00:07:31,200
Take care of yourself, okay?

144
00:07:32,400 --> 00:07:32,960
Thanks once again.

145
00:07:33,680 --> 00:07:35,300
Hey, where's my wallet?

146
00:07:38,710 --> 00:07:39,770
I love it when we do this.

147
00:07:40,160 --> 00:07:41,160
So many words.

148
00:07:41,160 --> 00:07:42,350
So much language to take away.

149
00:07:43,040 --> 00:07:43,230
That's right.

150
00:07:44,040 --> 00:07:52,660
So now we're going to take a look at the second part of language takeaway where we have another set of four words that we want to take a look at.

151
00:07:52,660 --> 00:07:53,000
Language takeaway.

152
00:07:54,310 --> 00:07:54,610
All right.

153
00:07:54,710 --> 00:07:56,210
The first is actually two words.

154
00:07:56,490 --> 00:07:57,950
We have two opposites.

155
00:07:58,370 --> 00:07:58,670
Okay?

156
00:07:59,130 --> 00:08:01,290
So you said earlier, the jury has to make a decision.

157
00:08:02,410 --> 00:08:06,520
The jury has to decide, is the defendant innocent or guilty?

158
00:08:06,980 --> 00:08:07,300
Okay.

159
00:08:07,720 --> 00:08:09,220
These are two important adjectives.

160
00:08:09,320 --> 00:08:10,320
Innocent.

161
00:08:10,320 --> 00:08:11,320
What does this mean?

162
00:08:11,320 --> 00:08:13,740
So if he is innocent, that means he did not commit the crime.

163
00:08:14,120 --> 00:08:16,420
He is not responsible for what happened.

164
00:08:16,560 --> 00:08:17,900
He did not steal the wallets.

165
00:08:18,500 --> 00:08:18,800
Okay.

166
00:08:18,800 --> 00:08:20,500
So we often say babies are so innocent.

167
00:08:20,920 --> 00:08:21,920
Right.

168
00:08:21,920 --> 00:08:22,920
They can't do anything.

169
00:08:22,920 --> 00:08:23,220
They can't do anything wrong.

170
00:08:23,920 --> 00:08:24,920
Right.

171
00:08:24,920 --> 00:08:26,400
So guilty would be the opposite.

172
00:08:26,400 --> 00:08:26,560
That's right.

173
00:08:27,400 --> 00:08:34,430
So usually when someone is guilty, they're sad, they feel bad, you want them to pay you.

174
00:08:34,840 --> 00:08:35,330
And they go to jail.

175
00:08:35,850 --> 00:08:35,970
Yeah.

176
00:08:36,190 --> 00:08:37,970
In bad situations, they go to jail.

177
00:08:38,590 --> 00:08:40,610
If you burn down my house, of course you're going to go to jail.

178
00:08:40,790 --> 00:08:41,250
Right.

179
00:08:41,840 --> 00:08:42,840
All right.

180
00:08:42,840 --> 00:08:43,840
So guilty means you did a crime.

181
00:08:43,840 --> 00:08:44,630
You did a bad thing.

182
00:08:45,440 --> 00:08:48,780
So we talked about the prosecution and the defense of the defendant.

183
00:08:49,880 --> 00:08:56,200
And what happens is they also present some key elements that will prove if the person is innocent or guilty.

184
00:08:56,960 --> 00:08:59,560
And those elements are called evidence.

185
00:08:59,560 --> 00:08:59,680
That's right.

186
00:09:00,560 --> 00:09:02,480
So back to this idea, we tell a story.

187
00:09:02,480 --> 00:09:04,240
Maybe a witness can help us tell our story.

188
00:09:04,540 --> 00:09:06,460
But as you say, evidence is stuff.

189
00:09:07,160 --> 00:09:07,480
It's things.

190
00:09:08,060 --> 00:09:13,480
So in the case of the house, I could say you have matches and you smell like smoke and oil.

191
00:09:13,940 --> 00:09:17,140
This is evidence for me to use to show that you are guilty.

192
00:09:17,440 --> 00:09:18,440
Right.

193
00:09:18,440 --> 00:09:20,520
So in this case, do we have any evidence?

194
00:09:20,520 --> 00:09:23,180
There's no evidence, says the defendant.

195
00:09:24,540 --> 00:09:24,760
Right.

196
00:09:25,160 --> 00:09:25,300
All right.

197
00:09:25,460 --> 00:09:26,440
So evidence is proof.

198
00:09:26,720 --> 00:09:28,400
It's physical proof.

199
00:09:28,400 --> 00:09:29,400
Yeah.

200
00:09:29,400 --> 00:09:37,730
And most of the times when you see movies or TV shows, they will find, I don't know, like maybe a fingerprint that's evidence or somebody's glasses.

201
00:09:38,240 --> 00:09:39,240
A cell phone.

202
00:09:39,240 --> 00:09:40,240
Exactly.

203
00:09:40,240 --> 00:09:41,240
All right.

204
00:09:41,240 --> 00:09:42,240
So evidence.

205
00:09:42,240 --> 00:09:43,960
This is very important when you want to build a case.

206
00:09:43,960 --> 00:09:47,160
Finally, at the end of a trial, we have a verdict.

207
00:09:47,310 --> 00:09:48,410
Now, what is a verdict?

208
00:09:49,320 --> 00:09:57,960
Well a verdict is a final decision or a conclusion in which the jury says, yeah, the person is guilty or he's not guilty, he's innocent.

209
00:09:57,960 --> 00:10:01,360
So we use this word usually to reach a verdict.

210
00:10:01,360 --> 00:10:03,480
The jury reaches a verdict.

211
00:10:03,480 --> 00:10:07,410
Now it's interesting why we say to reach because a jury is not just one person.

212
00:10:07,760 --> 00:10:09,520
A jury is many people.

213
00:10:09,520 --> 00:10:11,770
And so these people have to decide together.

214
00:10:12,090 --> 00:10:12,750
They have to argue.

215
00:10:13,210 --> 00:10:16,150
They have to make a conclusion that everyone agrees on.

216
00:10:16,520 --> 00:10:21,720
And this is to reach a conclusion, to reach a verdict because they have to travel there.

217
00:10:21,720 --> 00:10:23,880
They have to go together and figure it out.

218
00:10:23,880 --> 00:10:24,880
Right.

219
00:10:24,880 --> 00:10:25,310
That's very, very important.

220
00:10:25,880 --> 00:10:29,220
So the jury reaches a verdict.

221
00:10:29,220 --> 00:10:31,230
And we did have one other phrase that's very interesting.

222
00:10:31,440 --> 00:10:32,770
It doesn't quite fit with the other ones.

223
00:10:33,370 --> 00:10:35,730
We heard this phrase from the defendant.

224
00:10:36,520 --> 00:10:41,430
The lawyer says, so these so-called witnesses didn't actually see anything.

225
00:10:41,680 --> 00:10:43,800
What is so-called?

226
00:10:43,800 --> 00:10:47,670
So he's being sarcastic when he uses this phrase, so-called, right?

227
00:10:48,440 --> 00:10:51,930
That's why even his tone changes, these so-called witnesses.

228
00:10:52,760 --> 00:10:55,610
So it's a way of expressing sarcasm.

229
00:10:55,880 --> 00:11:00,540
He's saying they say they're witnesses, but they're not really because they didn't see anything.

230
00:11:01,440 --> 00:11:02,440
Right.

231
00:11:02,440 --> 00:11:04,600
So so-called means you say that they are, but they're not really.

232
00:11:04,740 --> 00:11:04,980
Right.

233
00:11:05,600 --> 00:11:09,680
I could also say, your so-called lawyer thinks he can help you, but he can't.

234
00:11:09,980 --> 00:11:10,100
Right.

235
00:11:10,380 --> 00:11:12,700
That means I don't think your lawyer is very good.

236
00:11:12,880 --> 00:11:14,120
I don't think he's a real lawyer.

237
00:11:14,360 --> 00:11:14,500
Right.

238
00:11:15,400 --> 00:11:18,420
Or sometimes people say, my so-called friend stood me up last night.

239
00:11:18,840 --> 00:11:20,520
Oh, so it's not really a friend.

240
00:11:20,640 --> 00:11:21,640
He's not really a friend.

241
00:11:21,640 --> 00:11:21,840
Exactly.

242
00:11:22,640 --> 00:11:23,640
So-called.

243
00:11:23,640 --> 00:11:24,640
All right.

244
00:11:24,640 --> 00:11:25,640
That's another good one.

245
00:11:25,640 --> 00:11:26,520
Let's listen to this dialogue one more time.

246
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:30,100
Really think about some of the words you heard today, and we'll be back in a moment.

